WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:14.210 --> 00:00:16.900
- Hey everyone, Sal Khan
here from Khan Academy.

00:00:16.900 --> 00:00:19.540
Welcome to our daily homeroom live stream.

00:00:19.540 --> 00:00:23.050
If it's your first time and
are wondering what is this?

00:00:23.050 --> 00:00:25.180
This is a live stream
that we started doing

00:00:25.180 --> 00:00:27.660
every day since school
closure started happening

00:00:27.660 --> 00:00:30.800
'cause we realize as a not
for profit with a mission

00:00:30.800 --> 00:00:33.490
of free world class education
for anyone anywhere,

00:00:33.490 --> 00:00:35.740
we have a role to step up.

00:00:35.740 --> 00:00:39.070
It's our duty to step up to
support teachers, parents,

00:00:39.070 --> 00:00:41.740
and students as we all
navigate through this crisis.

00:00:41.740 --> 00:00:44.290
And so we've been trying
to put as many supports out

00:00:44.290 --> 00:00:47.151
as possible teacher
webinars, parent webinars,

00:00:47.151 --> 00:00:51.650
(mumbles) class schedules,
frameworks for learning plans

00:00:51.650 --> 00:00:53.980
for students, and also this homeroom.

00:00:53.980 --> 00:00:55.220
And this is a way to stay connected

00:00:55.220 --> 00:00:56.930
in times of social distancing

00:00:56.930 --> 00:00:59.010
and to just talk about interesting things.

00:00:59.010 --> 00:01:01.150
Many of which might be
related to the crisis

00:01:01.150 --> 00:01:03.910
or whatever else is on folks' mind.

00:01:03.910 --> 00:01:05.930
I do like to remind folks as I just did,

00:01:05.930 --> 00:01:07.100
that we are not for profit

00:01:07.100 --> 00:01:09.620
and we can only exist through
philanthropic donations.

00:01:09.620 --> 00:01:11.460
So if you're in a position to do so

00:01:11.460 --> 00:01:12.920
please think about donating,

00:01:12.920 --> 00:01:15.840
we were running at a deficit
even before the crisis

00:01:15.840 --> 00:01:17.890
and now we're seeing about two and a half

00:01:17.890 --> 00:01:20.150
to three times the
traffic we normally see,

00:01:20.150 --> 00:01:22.790
and you could imagine that
plus all of the other programs

00:01:22.790 --> 00:01:25.140
and content we're trying to accelerate

00:01:25.140 --> 00:01:27.360
are only making our costs go up higher.

00:01:27.360 --> 00:01:28.790
I do wanna give a special thanks

00:01:28.790 --> 00:01:30.840
to several corporate
partners that stepped up

00:01:30.840 --> 00:01:33.070
over the last couple of
weeks when they realized

00:01:33.070 --> 00:01:36.100
how big the need was to support everyone

00:01:36.100 --> 00:01:38.500
on the education front for COVID.

00:01:38.500 --> 00:01:41.220
Special thanks to Bank
of America, Google.org,

00:01:41.220 --> 00:01:44.120
Fastly, AT&amp;T and Novartis.

00:01:44.120 --> 00:01:45.520
They've made a huge difference,

00:01:45.520 --> 00:01:47.410
but we still are running at a deficit.

00:01:47.410 --> 00:01:49.390
So any more help from either corporations

00:01:49.390 --> 00:01:52.890
or individuals is very,
very much appreciated.

00:01:52.890 --> 00:01:56.590
So with that we'll get into
the heart of this live stream

00:01:56.590 --> 00:01:58.860
and sometimes we have guests
and sometimes we don't

00:01:58.860 --> 00:02:00.870
or I guess you could say
today I am the guest.

00:02:00.870 --> 00:02:03.190
And so we could treat
this livestream as really

00:02:03.190 --> 00:02:08.140
just a ask Sal anything so
you can get on the Facebook

00:02:08.140 --> 00:02:11.510
and YouTube message boards
and literally ask me anything

00:02:11.510 --> 00:02:12.990
and I have team members
that are helping me

00:02:12.990 --> 00:02:15.600
surface interesting questions.

00:02:15.600 --> 00:02:18.240
But we also said that
given that this is the end

00:02:18.240 --> 00:02:19.830
of Teacher Appreciation Week

00:02:19.830 --> 00:02:21.880
that we wanna make sure
teachers feel supported

00:02:21.880 --> 00:02:23.820
especially as they're
all trying to navigate

00:02:23.820 --> 00:02:26.670
this world of distance
learning or making videos.

00:02:26.670 --> 00:02:30.330
And so I'm also happy to
answer questions for teachers

00:02:30.330 --> 00:02:33.840
who are wondering what
are tips we might have

00:02:33.840 --> 00:02:36.280
for making content, for making videos

00:02:36.280 --> 00:02:38.220
and what are the tools we have?

00:02:38.220 --> 00:02:40.000
So feel free to put those questions in.

00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:42.870
I already have a question from Facebook

00:02:42.870 --> 00:02:45.127
Jay is asking,

00:02:45.127 --> 00:02:47.790
"I must admit that I would be happy

00:02:47.790 --> 00:02:49.880
if I just knew the name of the tablet

00:02:49.880 --> 00:02:51.560
that Sal uses to present.

00:02:51.560 --> 00:02:53.750
That is the biggest challenge
when teaching remotely.

00:02:53.750 --> 00:02:56.230
I cannot tell you how many
times teachers have said,

00:02:56.230 --> 00:02:59.210
if only we had tablets like Sal uses."

00:02:59.210 --> 00:03:01.380
All right I will tell you
the name of the tablet

00:03:01.380 --> 00:03:03.230
I actually have it right over here,

00:03:03.230 --> 00:03:05.480
and I'm not normally
about product endorsement.

00:03:05.480 --> 00:03:07.770
So I'm not sure if this is
the best tablet out there

00:03:07.770 --> 00:03:10.500
or what but it's definitely met my needs.

00:03:10.500 --> 00:03:15.500
So let's see this is the
Intuos Pro Medium Pen Tablet.

00:03:15.530 --> 00:03:20.530
I think it costs 100 or
$200, it looks like this,

00:03:20.830 --> 00:03:23.460
and it will come with a pen that...

00:03:23.460 --> 00:03:24.550
Whoops I'm pressing buttons on it,

00:03:24.550 --> 00:03:25.680
I hope (mumbles)...

00:03:25.680 --> 00:03:27.100
It comes with a pen that looks like this.

00:03:27.100 --> 00:03:29.370
This camera is kind of a reverse mode.

00:03:29.370 --> 00:03:31.020
So there you go.

00:03:31.020 --> 00:03:34.620
So that is the tablet and I
think the cost really increases

00:03:34.620 --> 00:03:37.870
as you go from the small,
to the medium, to the large.

00:03:37.870 --> 00:03:40.420
For many years at Khan Academy
I had the small version,

00:03:40.420 --> 00:03:42.307
which was I think was $70,

00:03:42.307 --> 00:03:44.441
and you can go pretty far with that.

00:03:44.441 --> 00:03:47.210
So yeah hopefully that is useful

00:03:47.210 --> 00:03:48.810
and obviously this is useful.

00:03:48.810 --> 00:03:49.970
Tablets like this are useful

00:03:49.970 --> 00:03:52.820
for making Khan Academy style videos.

00:03:52.820 --> 00:03:55.180
And I actually use a
tablet like that with a pen

00:03:55.180 --> 00:03:57.310
and I use Camtasia to record my screen

00:03:57.310 --> 00:04:00.060
and this is the microphone that I have.

00:04:00.060 --> 00:04:02.030
This is a Samson...

00:04:02.030 --> 00:04:03.130
And once again,

00:04:03.130 --> 00:04:04.610
I'm sure there's other good microphones

00:04:04.610 --> 00:04:08.880
let's see Samson CO3U,

00:04:08.880 --> 00:04:11.160
I just realized it
wouldn't make do much good

00:04:11.160 --> 00:04:13.980
if I'm reading the model number

00:04:13.980 --> 00:04:16.700
when my mouth is on the
other side of the microphone.

00:04:16.700 --> 00:04:18.340
But just with that and Camtasia

00:04:18.340 --> 00:04:20.750
you can make exactly the
types of videos I make.

00:04:20.750 --> 00:04:24.620
And obviously, this is
useful for all of y'all

00:04:24.620 --> 00:04:28.370
who are also doing live,
Zoom or Google Hangout

00:04:28.370 --> 00:04:30.920
type sessions as well 'cause
you can share your screen

00:04:30.920 --> 00:04:32.150
and then use your art program.

00:04:32.150 --> 00:04:35.890
Oh, and I use SketchBook
Pro for the art program,

00:04:35.890 --> 00:04:37.160
but you could use others.

00:04:37.160 --> 00:04:39.500
I like to just use something
that's quite responsive.

00:04:39.500 --> 00:04:41.700
Some of the art programs
that get super fancy

00:04:41.700 --> 00:04:43.870
there's a little bit of a
lag and that bothers me.

00:04:43.870 --> 00:04:45.390
So that's what I try to do,

00:04:45.390 --> 00:04:48.300
and I've been working with some teachers

00:04:48.300 --> 00:04:50.150
running some live sessions as well.

00:04:50.150 --> 00:04:51.990
And so I use the exact same tools for that

00:04:51.990 --> 00:04:54.300
where I can share my screen
and share the art program.

00:04:54.300 --> 00:04:57.220
I will say a low tech hack
for the live sessions,

00:04:57.220 --> 00:04:58.350
and actually in theory you could do this

00:04:58.350 --> 00:05:00.020
for your videos as well,

00:05:00.020 --> 00:05:03.460
is those those small whiteboards
that we have students

00:05:03.460 --> 00:05:04.620
do their problem on,

00:05:04.620 --> 00:05:06.070
you can literally just have one of those

00:05:06.070 --> 00:05:08.190
and write with a with a marker.

00:05:08.190 --> 00:05:10.320
I've actually found that
to be reasonably good

00:05:10.320 --> 00:05:13.170
especially for the kind
of the live sessions

00:05:13.170 --> 00:05:15.840
when you're trying to work with students.

00:05:15.840 --> 00:05:18.537
So from YouTube Matthew Masters asks,

00:05:18.537 --> 00:05:20.850
"Sal, do you think there
will always be a place

00:05:20.850 --> 00:05:22.070
for classroom learning

00:05:22.070 --> 00:05:25.530
or will online learning
eventually become the standard?"

00:05:25.530 --> 00:05:28.000
Simple answer is yes I think

00:05:28.000 --> 00:05:30.430
there will always be a place
for classroom learning,

00:05:30.430 --> 00:05:31.960
or I hope there will always be a place

00:05:31.960 --> 00:05:33.170
for classroom learning.

00:05:33.170 --> 00:05:38.170
Especially for students who
haven't reached adulthood.

00:05:39.020 --> 00:05:42.220
So I would say for K through 12 for sure,

00:05:42.220 --> 00:05:46.700
because in theory yes students could learn

00:05:46.700 --> 00:05:48.960
a lot of the academic skills

00:05:48.960 --> 00:05:50.670
potentially on their own time and pace,

00:05:50.670 --> 00:05:52.730
on things like Khan Academy,

00:05:52.730 --> 00:05:54.970
and or you can have Zoom sessions

00:05:54.970 --> 00:05:58.230
and you can have this kind
of distance type of learning,

00:05:58.230 --> 00:06:00.050
but that's only part of what

00:06:00.050 --> 00:06:02.650
really makes the school
experience powerful.

00:06:02.650 --> 00:06:04.400
There is a whole other level

00:06:04.400 --> 00:06:06.460
of having incredible teacher mentors,

00:06:06.460 --> 00:06:08.210
being able to form bonds with them,

00:06:08.210 --> 00:06:09.930
being able to do
extracurricular activities,

00:06:09.930 --> 00:06:13.050
all of the socialization
that goes on in school,

00:06:13.050 --> 00:06:14.640
knowing how to work in groups,

00:06:14.640 --> 00:06:16.920
knowing when to communicate,

00:06:16.920 --> 00:06:19.940
when not to communicate, know
how to get your point across.

00:06:19.940 --> 00:06:21.840
These are all super important skills

00:06:21.840 --> 00:06:24.400
that you'll never see on
any standardized test,

00:06:24.400 --> 00:06:27.330
but we all know that
they're very, very important

00:06:27.330 --> 00:06:28.930
for overall life and success.

00:06:28.930 --> 00:06:30.840
So, I've always said If I had to pick

00:06:30.840 --> 00:06:32.860
between an amazing teacher
and amazing technology

00:06:32.860 --> 00:06:34.740
I would pick the amazing
teacher every time

00:06:34.740 --> 00:06:37.410
and I would prefer to have
that amazing teacher in person,

00:06:37.410 --> 00:06:40.150
so we should definitely strive for that.

00:06:40.150 --> 00:06:42.140
And I think I would want
it for my own children

00:06:42.140 --> 00:06:44.610
and frankly all children.

00:06:44.610 --> 00:06:47.190
And so I think the interesting question

00:06:47.190 --> 00:06:49.330
over the COVID crisis is,

00:06:49.330 --> 00:06:51.310
how do we get as close to that as possible

00:06:51.310 --> 00:06:55.170
given the constraints that
we have of social distancing?

00:06:55.170 --> 00:06:56.750
And then longer term,

00:06:56.750 --> 00:06:59.210
hopefully when we get out of
this crisis sooner than later.

00:06:59.210 --> 00:07:01.460
How do we leverage the
best of both worlds?

00:07:01.460 --> 00:07:04.230
So I think hopefully we're
gonna go back to school,

00:07:04.230 --> 00:07:05.770
kids are gonna be to
get that socialization,

00:07:05.770 --> 00:07:07.060
they're gonna be back in those classrooms

00:07:07.060 --> 00:07:08.560
with their amazing teachers,

00:07:08.560 --> 00:07:11.390
but then everyone has
built a stronger muscle

00:07:12.270 --> 00:07:14.790
for sometimes maybe the
distance learning does work

00:07:14.790 --> 00:07:18.950
maybe over summers, maybe over
breaks, maybe after school.

00:07:18.950 --> 00:07:20.370
It doesn't have to just be

00:07:20.370 --> 00:07:21.450
between the teachers and the students,

00:07:21.450 --> 00:07:23.780
it could be older students
and younger students

00:07:23.780 --> 00:07:25.300
or it could be the peers working together,

00:07:25.300 --> 00:07:26.570
so I think that would be a good takeaway

00:07:26.570 --> 00:07:28.110
from this whole process.

00:07:28.110 --> 00:07:31.990
I think higher education,
there are some real questions.

00:07:31.990 --> 00:07:33.440
I loved my college experience,

00:07:33.440 --> 00:07:35.900
I loved sitting in the
dorm and pontificating

00:07:35.900 --> 00:07:37.620
about philosophical questions

00:07:37.620 --> 00:07:40.710
and I met my wife and many of
my best friends in college.

00:07:40.710 --> 00:07:43.170
So I definitely think that was
a very valuable experience,

00:07:43.170 --> 00:07:44.310
but there's a lot of students

00:07:44.310 --> 00:07:46.363
who, there's huge trade offs.

00:07:47.250 --> 00:07:49.470
They need to help support their families,

00:07:49.470 --> 00:07:52.620
they might want you to start make money

00:07:52.620 --> 00:07:54.200
while they're in college,

00:07:54.200 --> 00:07:56.130
and so I think in cases like that

00:07:56.130 --> 00:07:58.264
when just you have more of adult learners,

00:07:58.264 --> 00:08:00.900
I think there will always be a place

00:08:00.900 --> 00:08:03.370
for the in person college experience.

00:08:03.370 --> 00:08:05.430
But I can imagine some adult learners

00:08:05.430 --> 00:08:06.950
decide that that's not for them

00:08:06.950 --> 00:08:08.540
and that they could still
get their college degree

00:08:08.540 --> 00:08:09.960
and learn all the material,

00:08:09.960 --> 00:08:11.570
but do it at their own time and pace

00:08:11.570 --> 00:08:12.790
and be a little bit more flexible

00:08:12.790 --> 00:08:13.970
because maybe they hold a job

00:08:13.970 --> 00:08:16.180
or they have to support their
family and things like that.

00:08:16.180 --> 00:08:21.060
So, my personal view is I
hope we only double down

00:08:21.060 --> 00:08:23.900
on the physical experience
but leverage some of the tools

00:08:23.900 --> 00:08:27.280
we're all exploring with
a little bit more as well.

00:08:27.280 --> 00:08:30.037
So from Facebook, Jaya Gupta asks,

00:08:30.037 --> 00:08:31.940
"How can I become a good teacher?

00:08:31.940 --> 00:08:33.160
Because I'm teaching my students,

00:08:33.160 --> 00:08:34.890
but some students are
not taking it seriously.

00:08:34.890 --> 00:08:37.207
Please tell me dear sir, what to do."

00:08:38.830 --> 00:08:42.100
Jaya, I won't pretend like
I have all of the answers.

00:08:42.100 --> 00:08:45.880
And I in the Khan Academy journey

00:08:45.880 --> 00:08:47.700
I've done a certain flavor of teaching,

00:08:47.700 --> 00:08:49.700
but there's a whole other
dimension of teaching

00:08:49.700 --> 00:08:52.390
which you and many other amazing teachers

00:08:52.390 --> 00:08:53.720
are doing on a day to day basis

00:08:53.720 --> 00:08:55.440
that I think I can learn a ton from y'all

00:08:55.440 --> 00:08:57.780
versus the other way around.

00:08:57.780 --> 00:09:02.560
My best advice, though, is try to make it

00:09:02.560 --> 00:09:04.310
as interactive as possible,

00:09:04.310 --> 00:09:06.587
and when you do that think about it.

00:09:06.587 --> 00:09:08.350
And this actually goes
to the earlier question

00:09:08.350 --> 00:09:11.090
about in person versus digital.

00:09:11.090 --> 00:09:12.850
I think there'll always
be a place for in person,

00:09:12.850 --> 00:09:15.350
but we should always be
conscientious of what maybe digital,

00:09:15.350 --> 00:09:18.350
or online, or software,
or videos could do.

00:09:18.350 --> 00:09:21.910
So, if you're just giving a
lecture for 10 minutes straight

00:09:21.910 --> 00:09:24.290
in theory, that could happen over a video.

00:09:24.290 --> 00:09:26.510
And so if that could happen over video

00:09:26.510 --> 00:09:28.140
then maybe it should happen over video,

00:09:28.140 --> 00:09:30.610
but then that liberates
you to do something else.

00:09:30.610 --> 00:09:33.090
And it's not like all of a
sudden there's nothing to do,

00:09:33.090 --> 00:09:34.670
you could have a Socratic dialogue,

00:09:34.670 --> 00:09:35.730
you could have a simulation,

00:09:35.730 --> 00:09:37.010
you could have a project,

00:09:37.010 --> 00:09:39.450
you could put a problem up on the board

00:09:39.450 --> 00:09:41.020
that's a little bit more challenging

00:09:41.020 --> 00:09:43.410
and have the students
struggle with it a little bit.

00:09:43.410 --> 00:09:44.810
Have them break into groups

00:09:44.810 --> 00:09:46.450
and try to come up with their own answers,

00:09:46.450 --> 00:09:49.260
and then present it and you're
walking around nudging them,

00:09:49.260 --> 00:09:51.950
giving them a little hint
for the ones that get stuck.

00:09:51.950 --> 00:09:54.360
So I think there's a huge opportunity

00:09:54.360 --> 00:09:56.020
for that classroom to just interact

00:09:56.020 --> 00:09:57.690
with more with each other.

00:09:57.690 --> 00:09:59.960
Those students that
you might be describing

00:09:59.960 --> 00:10:02.060
whose eyes are glazed over a little bit,

00:10:02.060 --> 00:10:04.047
I generally think if
you told that student,

00:10:04.047 --> 00:10:05.710
"Hey, pair up with the person next to you

00:10:05.710 --> 00:10:08.940
or form a group of three
or four, here's a puzzle,

00:10:08.940 --> 00:10:10.580
here's an interesting challenge,

00:10:10.580 --> 00:10:12.710
here's a philosophical question."

00:10:12.710 --> 00:10:16.890
To ponder, their eyes will
light up and in a lot of ways

00:10:16.890 --> 00:10:20.080
it can take a little bit
of the burden off of you.

00:10:20.080 --> 00:10:22.360
I've definitely sat in
front of a classroom

00:10:22.360 --> 00:10:27.360
of students and tried to
teach them and you're like,

00:10:27.547 --> 00:10:28.520
"What are they thinking?

00:10:28.520 --> 00:10:32.030
They're staring at me, I
have to be on for the entire

00:10:32.030 --> 00:10:32.900
40, 50 minutes."

00:10:32.900 --> 00:10:36.530
It's incredibly draining both
physically and emotionally,

00:10:36.530 --> 00:10:39.240
and as soon as you kind of get out

00:10:39.240 --> 00:10:42.380
of that kinda being
the person on the stage

00:10:42.380 --> 00:10:44.880
so to speak and then you're
kind of with the students

00:10:44.880 --> 00:10:48.280
and you're tackling
something with them together.

00:10:48.280 --> 00:10:52.383
It actually allows you to
not have to feel as on,

00:10:53.450 --> 00:10:56.630
which I've actually personally found good.

00:10:56.630 --> 00:10:57.970
People might be surprised to hear,

00:10:57.970 --> 00:11:02.094
I'm very much an introvert I
can pretend to be an extrovert

00:11:02.094 --> 00:11:04.700
(laughs) like I do on this live stream,

00:11:04.700 --> 00:11:07.280
but I recharge my energy

00:11:07.280 --> 00:11:10.350
when I'm having one on one
conversations with people,

00:11:10.350 --> 00:11:12.010
when I'm able to go a little bit deeper,

00:11:12.010 --> 00:11:13.390
when I have a little bit more quiet time.

00:11:13.390 --> 00:11:15.730
And so for me it's incredibly draining

00:11:15.730 --> 00:11:18.490
to be on for long periods of time,

00:11:18.490 --> 00:11:21.240
but it's very energizing if
I can sit next to a student

00:11:21.240 --> 00:11:22.860
and work with them that way.

00:11:22.860 --> 00:11:25.660
So those are my tips make it
as interactive as possible,

00:11:25.660 --> 00:11:27.360
Have the students help each other,

00:11:28.484 --> 00:11:32.610
and things like Socratic
dialogue, simulations, games,

00:11:32.610 --> 00:11:34.840
challenging, make it active.

00:11:34.840 --> 00:11:37.573
I think would move everything
in the right direction.

00:11:38.530 --> 00:11:41.327
So from Facebook Alicia Moody says,

00:11:41.327 --> 00:11:43.110
"The hardest part of
this as a math teacher

00:11:43.110 --> 00:11:44.740
is supporting my most struggling students

00:11:44.740 --> 00:11:46.430
who are already behind.

00:11:46.430 --> 00:11:47.680
I teach high schoolers,

00:11:47.680 --> 00:11:49.320
some of whom have critical thinking skills

00:11:49.320 --> 00:11:51.340
closer to fourth to sixth grade.

00:11:51.340 --> 00:11:52.890
What are your favorite
supports for students

00:11:52.890 --> 00:11:53.810
struggling with connecting

00:11:53.810 --> 00:11:56.400
the abstractions of algebra with real life

00:11:56.400 --> 00:11:58.410
when they don't have much
foundational number sense,

00:11:58.410 --> 00:12:00.910
any online games or
basics Focused activities

00:12:00.910 --> 00:12:02.820
targeted towards teens?"

00:12:02.820 --> 00:12:05.290
Alicia or Alicia,

00:12:05.290 --> 00:12:08.114
You're asking a central question.

00:12:08.114 --> 00:12:11.210
And this is something we think
a lot about at Khan Academy

00:12:11.210 --> 00:12:13.337
because you've probably
heard me talk about it,

00:12:13.337 --> 00:12:17.000
and the traditional model
kids keep moving forward,

00:12:17.000 --> 00:12:19.450
they get to C on one test a D on another,

00:12:19.450 --> 00:12:22.490
that 20% that 30% gap,
those things accumulate

00:12:22.490 --> 00:12:24.220
and then they get to your classroom,

00:12:24.220 --> 00:12:26.550
and as you mentioned
they're kind of operating

00:12:26.550 --> 00:12:27.383
at a...

00:12:27.383 --> 00:12:28.814
At least they're ready for the math

00:12:28.814 --> 00:12:31.490
at a fourth through sixth grade level.

00:12:31.490 --> 00:12:33.180
So there's a couple things we've done,

00:12:33.180 --> 00:12:36.230
we've seen and really this is
learning from other teachers,

00:12:36.230 --> 00:12:37.430
we've seen teachers

00:12:37.430 --> 00:12:39.460
especially teachers who
are serving students

00:12:39.460 --> 00:12:41.260
who might be many grade levels behind

00:12:41.260 --> 00:12:43.250
who haven't maybe had
gotten all the supports

00:12:43.250 --> 00:12:46.560
they might need to start
them at the beginning.

00:12:46.560 --> 00:12:48.300
So, I'll tell you what you could do today

00:12:48.300 --> 00:12:49.860
and then I'll tell you
what I hope you can do

00:12:49.860 --> 00:12:50.980
in about two months,

00:12:50.980 --> 00:12:52.770
'cause we're working on
some things that I think

00:12:52.770 --> 00:12:54.430
will help solve this problem.

00:12:54.430 --> 00:12:56.670
So today, I would actually
have those students

00:12:56.670 --> 00:13:01.120
start on our arithmetic course

00:13:01.120 --> 00:13:02.700
and just go from the beginning,

00:13:02.700 --> 00:13:05.450
from one plus one straight
through the arithmetic course.

00:13:05.450 --> 00:13:07.560
And they can use course challenges

00:13:07.560 --> 00:13:09.060
and unit test to accelerate.

00:13:09.060 --> 00:13:10.830
So what I would suggest
is have those students

00:13:10.830 --> 00:13:13.660
start with the course challenge
on the arithmetic course,

00:13:13.660 --> 00:13:17.150
and then the stuff they
got right, that's great,

00:13:17.150 --> 00:13:18.510
and then the stuff they got wrong

00:13:18.510 --> 00:13:20.360
they can go into those
units and remediate.

00:13:20.360 --> 00:13:22.870
And if they're getting 70,
80% on the course challenge

00:13:22.870 --> 00:13:23.900
they should keep taking it,

00:13:23.900 --> 00:13:26.000
and then they'll get to be able to show

00:13:26.000 --> 00:13:27.420
the mastery on those skills,

00:13:27.420 --> 00:13:29.010
and then they can go back and get mastery

00:13:29.010 --> 00:13:30.730
on the things that they
need to fill in those gaps.

00:13:30.730 --> 00:13:31.890
And then they should do the same thing

00:13:31.890 --> 00:13:33.370
with our pre algebra course,

00:13:33.370 --> 00:13:36.490
and if they do that it's kind
of going slow to go fast,

00:13:36.490 --> 00:13:38.220
they're gonna have a
very strong foundation,

00:13:38.220 --> 00:13:39.838
they're actually gonna build
a lot of their confidence

00:13:39.838 --> 00:13:41.520
and you're gonna have a lot more fluency,

00:13:41.520 --> 00:13:44.700
and they're gonna be ready
for your algebra class.

00:13:44.700 --> 00:13:48.277
A lot of times when a student
in an algebra class says,

00:13:48.277 --> 00:13:50.777
"Hey, teacher, when am I
gonna need this in life?"

00:13:51.630 --> 00:13:53.700
I think they might be asking
when are they gonna need it,

00:13:53.700 --> 00:13:55.330
but it also might be a defense mechanism.

00:13:55.330 --> 00:13:57.950
In some ways they're trying
to protect their self-esteem

00:13:57.950 --> 00:13:59.493
that this doesn't matter,

00:14:00.670 --> 00:14:03.430
because they're lost, they
have a gap in decimals,

00:14:03.430 --> 00:14:04.730
they have a gap in negative numbers,

00:14:04.730 --> 00:14:06.560
and all of a sudden there's
an equation on the board

00:14:06.560 --> 00:14:08.320
that has negative numbers and decimals

00:14:08.320 --> 00:14:10.320
and even if they're aware
that they have those gaps

00:14:10.320 --> 00:14:11.230
they might be embarrassed

00:14:11.230 --> 00:14:13.470
'cause they know it was fourth
or fifth grade material.

00:14:13.470 --> 00:14:16.627
And so I think the best
way to short circuit that,

00:14:16.627 --> 00:14:18.670
"Hey teacher when am I
gonna need to know this?"

00:14:18.670 --> 00:14:20.600
is by making sure kids have that fluency

00:14:20.600 --> 00:14:22.580
and that strong foundation
from the beginning

00:14:22.580 --> 00:14:24.780
and they have as few gaps as possible.

00:14:24.780 --> 00:14:26.150
And the example I show

00:14:26.150 --> 00:14:30.810
is that when you're in PE
class, when I was in ninth grade

00:14:30.810 --> 00:14:34.770
and I wasn't the strongest
basketball player,

00:14:34.770 --> 00:14:36.397
but I didn't ask the coach,

00:14:36.397 --> 00:14:38.210
"Hey coach, when am I
going to have to throw

00:14:38.210 --> 00:14:41.060
a orange round ball into
a 10 foot hoop in life,

00:14:41.060 --> 00:14:42.150
when do I need to know this?"

00:14:42.150 --> 00:14:43.590
And the reason why I didn't ask that

00:14:43.590 --> 00:14:46.140
is even though I wasn't the best player

00:14:46.140 --> 00:14:47.890
I was able to engage,

00:14:47.890 --> 00:14:50.853
it was interactive to
the previous question,

00:14:50.853 --> 00:14:52.930
and I was enjoying myself.

00:14:52.930 --> 00:14:56.110
And so I'm a big believer
that if most students

00:14:56.110 --> 00:14:59.380
are able to fill in their gaps
and feel like they can engage

00:14:59.380 --> 00:15:01.680
all of a sudden they
actually will get excited

00:15:01.680 --> 00:15:03.150
about that topic.

00:15:03.150 --> 00:15:06.307
Now, a very good answer when someone says,

00:15:06.307 --> 00:15:08.080
"When will I have to use this?"

00:15:08.080 --> 00:15:10.607
Sometimes the teacher we
sometimes hem and haw and say,

00:15:10.607 --> 00:15:12.390
"Well, if you become
an engineer you'll..."

00:15:12.390 --> 00:15:13.223
And then the student goes,

00:15:13.223 --> 00:15:14.056
"Well, I'm not gonna be an engineer."

00:15:14.056 --> 00:15:15.087
"Well, if you're going to finance."

00:15:15.087 --> 00:15:16.247
"Well, I'm not gonna go into finance."

00:15:16.247 --> 00:15:17.747
"Well, if you go into
medicine or medical research."

00:15:17.747 --> 00:15:18.580
"Well, I'm not."

00:15:18.580 --> 00:15:19.413
And you know...

00:15:19.413 --> 00:15:20.410
"Well, if you become a lawyer

00:15:20.410 --> 00:15:21.850
you need these critical thinking skills."

00:15:21.850 --> 00:15:24.134
And you can imagine it
becomes this kind of duel

00:15:24.134 --> 00:15:27.120
(laughs) between the
student and the teacher.

00:15:27.120 --> 00:15:29.530
I find just tell them straight up,

00:15:29.530 --> 00:15:31.610
the world is gonna evaluate you by this

00:15:31.610 --> 00:15:33.350
and it look, you can believe me or not,

00:15:33.350 --> 00:15:34.910
but these critical thinking skills

00:15:34.910 --> 00:15:37.190
are going be super valuable.

00:15:37.190 --> 00:15:39.790
There was one of the early
funders of Khan Academy

00:15:39.790 --> 00:15:42.310
he ran a large restaurant chain,

00:15:42.310 --> 00:15:45.540
and he told me that he
divides his employees

00:15:45.540 --> 00:15:47.630
by folks who understand algebra

00:15:47.630 --> 00:15:49.850
and folks who do not understand algebra.

00:15:49.850 --> 00:15:51.980
And that doesn't mean that you need to do

00:15:51.980 --> 00:15:54.610
a lot of deep algebra if you're
the manager of a restaurant,

00:15:54.610 --> 00:15:55.810
or if you're the district manager,

00:15:55.810 --> 00:15:57.300
or if you're the head of marketing,

00:15:57.300 --> 00:16:00.740
but what that told me and
it makes a lot of sense is,

00:16:00.740 --> 00:16:03.190
for him the folks who knew algebra

00:16:03.190 --> 00:16:07.150
had the critical thinking
skills to tackle complex things,

00:16:07.150 --> 00:16:09.210
simplify them deductively,

00:16:09.210 --> 00:16:11.510
make logical deductions about things.

00:16:11.510 --> 00:16:14.190
But whenever I tell that story to anyone

00:16:14.190 --> 00:16:15.277
who is kind of like,

00:16:15.277 --> 00:16:16.410
"When am I gonna need the algebra?"

00:16:16.410 --> 00:16:17.243
They're like,

00:16:17.243 --> 00:16:18.160
"Oh, wow."

00:16:18.160 --> 00:16:19.978
You know, my future bosses (laughs)...

00:16:19.978 --> 00:16:21.460
I wanna be... (mumbles)

00:16:21.460 --> 00:16:23.960
When they divide the world into
the people who know algebra

00:16:23.960 --> 00:16:25.310
and the people who don't know algebra,

00:16:25.310 --> 00:16:27.540
and the people who know algebra
get a lot more opportunity

00:16:27.540 --> 00:16:29.140
and a lot more income,

00:16:29.140 --> 00:16:31.800
I wanna be divided into the
people who know algebra.

00:16:31.800 --> 00:16:33.850
And it's also frankly just gonna give you

00:16:33.850 --> 00:16:35.330
a lot more confidence in life

00:16:35.330 --> 00:16:36.580
because you know that you're gonna be able

00:16:36.580 --> 00:16:39.343
to tackle problems and
deductively reason about things.

00:16:40.520 --> 00:16:44.247
So from Facebook Jane Molnar asked

00:16:44.247 --> 00:16:47.860
"Sal, do you have any tips
for working at a distance

00:16:47.860 --> 00:16:50.590
on Khan Academy with
children who cannot yet read,

00:16:50.590 --> 00:16:52.700
but are too old for the
preschool Khan Academy?

00:16:52.700 --> 00:16:53.700
Thank you."

00:16:53.700 --> 00:16:57.530
So Jane, you kind of just
described my youngest child

00:16:57.530 --> 00:16:59.340
and what I would encourage is

00:16:59.340 --> 00:17:01.220
take a second look at Khan Academy kids.

00:17:01.220 --> 00:17:02.130
And the reason why I'm telling you that

00:17:02.130 --> 00:17:03.840
is 'cause I recently took a second look

00:17:03.840 --> 00:17:05.300
at Khan Academy kids.

00:17:05.300 --> 00:17:07.080
The first version of Khan Academy kids

00:17:07.080 --> 00:17:09.060
starts with the letters, the numbers,

00:17:09.060 --> 00:17:10.830
and it kind of went through the pre-K

00:17:10.830 --> 00:17:13.180
and the K and the kindergarten standards.

00:17:13.180 --> 00:17:15.740
And my son had already kinda known that

00:17:15.740 --> 00:17:17.187
and when I was putting that
in front of him he's like,

00:17:17.187 --> 00:17:19.530
"Oh, this is easy, this is baby stuff."

00:17:19.530 --> 00:17:21.790
What's cool is in the last month

00:17:21.790 --> 00:17:24.860
Khan Academy kids just launched
the first grade standards,

00:17:24.860 --> 00:17:27.790
and I'm finding that that is
really hitting the sweet spot

00:17:27.790 --> 00:17:29.126
for my five and a half year old

00:17:29.126 --> 00:17:32.630
'cause he's learning to read
he's not fluent at reading yet,

00:17:32.630 --> 00:17:34.760
and it's really hitting
exactly where he needs to be

00:17:34.760 --> 00:17:38.160
and I'm guessing that's where your child

00:17:38.160 --> 00:17:39.290
might be ready for as well.

00:17:39.290 --> 00:17:40.930
So go on to Khan Academy kids

00:17:40.930 --> 00:17:44.150
you can actually change the
grade setting for your child,

00:17:44.150 --> 00:17:47.592
and now there's actually teacher accounts

00:17:47.592 --> 00:17:49.490
and which you can also access as a parent,

00:17:49.490 --> 00:17:50.970
where you can go to your account

00:17:50.970 --> 00:17:52.580
and if you click on your username

00:17:52.580 --> 00:17:53.970
it'll say become a teacher at Khan,

00:17:53.970 --> 00:17:55.850
and you can say you're
teaching first grade.

00:17:55.850 --> 00:17:58.370
And then you can actually
assign first grade content

00:17:58.370 --> 00:17:59.650
to your child as well.

00:17:59.650 --> 00:18:01.230
So, if your child's account

00:18:01.230 --> 00:18:03.000
gets turned into a first grade account

00:18:03.000 --> 00:18:05.430
then he or she will see
that first grade content

00:18:05.430 --> 00:18:09.130
and the system naturally
keeps track of where they are,

00:18:09.130 --> 00:18:11.450
and then makes the
recommendations accordingly

00:18:11.450 --> 00:18:13.320
across math, reading and writing.

00:18:13.320 --> 00:18:16.710
And then if you're a teacher or a parent

00:18:16.710 --> 00:18:18.620
you can also direct them
to certain activities.

00:18:18.620 --> 00:18:20.390
You can sample them and then direct them.

00:18:20.390 --> 00:18:22.710
So I highly take a second look at it

00:18:22.710 --> 00:18:27.290
'cause it'll get your child
through the basics of reading.

00:18:27.290 --> 00:18:28.750
So the fact that they're not reading yet,

00:18:28.750 --> 00:18:31.100
this is definitely going
to be useful for them.

00:18:31.950 --> 00:18:35.127
So YouTube, from YouTube, Alan Yab says,

00:18:35.127 --> 00:18:36.620
"Hi Sal, what do you think is better

00:18:36.620 --> 00:18:38.670
remote learning or in person?"

00:18:38.670 --> 00:18:41.240
Well, it's not a comparison

00:18:41.240 --> 00:18:43.400
I think they're good at different things.

00:18:43.400 --> 00:18:45.610
The ideal is in person,

00:18:45.610 --> 00:18:47.510
but what matters, you know...

00:18:48.461 --> 00:18:49.294
Let me put it this way,

00:18:49.294 --> 00:18:51.930
When you, you know, I remember
my in person college classes

00:18:51.930 --> 00:18:54.190
were 300 people in a lecture hall,

00:18:54.190 --> 00:18:55.790
or actually, maybe it was a lecture hall

00:18:55.790 --> 00:18:58.893
that could fit 300 people,
100 people showed up.

00:18:59.740 --> 00:19:01.590
The professor would
kind of give the lecture

00:19:01.590 --> 00:19:03.800
essentially read the lecture notes

00:19:03.800 --> 00:19:06.980
that he might have been
using for the last 18 years,

00:19:06.980 --> 00:19:09.630
and there's like 20 people in the front

00:19:09.630 --> 00:19:10.480
who were paying attention

00:19:10.480 --> 00:19:12.290
and then there's a group of
people someplace in the middle

00:19:12.290 --> 00:19:15.920
or the back and they're trying to stay up,

00:19:15.920 --> 00:19:18.100
but they're kind of
dozing off a little bit.

00:19:18.100 --> 00:19:20.600
That's not a great in person experience,

00:19:20.600 --> 00:19:23.000
and I could imagine a virtual experience

00:19:23.000 --> 00:19:25.300
that is at least as good
or better than that.

00:19:25.300 --> 00:19:26.530
If you just have a video of that

00:19:26.530 --> 00:19:28.540
you're already maybe as good

00:19:28.540 --> 00:19:29.960
because you can pause and repeat

00:19:29.960 --> 00:19:31.850
and watch it at two X speed.

00:19:31.850 --> 00:19:35.010
And then you can imagine a
Zoom session or Google Hangout

00:19:35.010 --> 00:19:37.530
where the professor is asking questions,

00:19:37.530 --> 00:19:39.750
having a Socratic dialogue,

00:19:39.750 --> 00:19:41.410
giving you a challenging problem

00:19:41.410 --> 00:19:44.160
and making you work in
groups on that problem

00:19:44.160 --> 00:19:45.900
before coming back to the professor.

00:19:45.900 --> 00:19:48.490
So that comparison

00:19:48.490 --> 00:19:51.380
is a highly not interactive
physical experience

00:19:51.380 --> 00:19:53.980
versus a highly interactive
virtual experience,

00:19:53.980 --> 00:19:55.820
in that world that virtual is better.

00:19:55.820 --> 00:19:57.220
Now, the dream scenario

00:19:57.220 --> 00:20:00.440
is a highly interactive
in person experience.

00:20:00.440 --> 00:20:02.250
So the ideal experience,

00:20:02.250 --> 00:20:03.320
what I want for my children,

00:20:03.320 --> 00:20:04.780
what I would want if I'm a student

00:20:04.780 --> 00:20:06.780
and frankly what I would want as a teacher

00:20:06.780 --> 00:20:08.670
is everyone's in the room,

00:20:08.670 --> 00:20:10.530
but it's not just me projecting something

00:20:10.530 --> 00:20:11.880
that could be a video,

00:20:11.880 --> 00:20:16.610
it's me facilitating, designing,
interactions, dialogue

00:20:16.610 --> 00:20:17.443
that's gonna form

00:20:17.443 --> 00:20:19.900
deep meaningful connections
with each other.

00:20:19.900 --> 00:20:21.370
So that's the real value

00:20:21.370 --> 00:20:24.320
of person to person in person interaction.

00:20:24.320 --> 00:20:26.240
Now, distance learning is
able to do a few things

00:20:26.240 --> 00:20:28.246
that you can't do in person,

00:20:28.246 --> 00:20:30.290
if students are traveling,

00:20:30.290 --> 00:20:33.240
obviously we're in an
emergency situation right now,

00:20:33.240 --> 00:20:35.510
distance learning can be more flexible

00:20:35.510 --> 00:20:39.530
around time and space, you
can run a class at 9:00 PM

00:20:39.530 --> 00:20:41.460
and it could include people
from all over the country

00:20:41.460 --> 00:20:42.800
or all over the world.

00:20:42.800 --> 00:20:44.960
So, it does introduce
some flexibility there.

00:20:44.960 --> 00:20:47.850
I think the ideal is a is
getting the best of both

00:20:47.850 --> 00:20:51.500
so that learning is never
bound by time or space,

00:20:51.500 --> 00:20:54.340
but you are for sure getting
that in person socialization,

00:20:54.340 --> 00:20:55.940
that in person connection,

00:20:55.940 --> 00:20:59.150
which is gonna be very
hard to replace by digital

00:20:59.150 --> 00:21:00.220
until we get...

00:21:01.060 --> 00:21:03.600
Until virtual reality probably...

00:21:03.600 --> 00:21:05.730
We have 50 years of
development of virtual reality

00:21:05.730 --> 00:21:07.203
before we can even get close.

00:21:08.260 --> 00:21:10.510
So let's see other questions.

00:21:10.510 --> 00:21:13.003
Let's see from YouTube.

00:21:13.003 --> 00:21:14.327
(mumbles) Kumar asks,

00:21:14.327 --> 00:21:17.200
"What are the books behind your back?"

00:21:17.200 --> 00:21:21.410
Well, there's all sorts
of books behind my back.

00:21:21.410 --> 00:21:23.010
This is actually the walk in closet

00:21:23.010 --> 00:21:26.040
where Khan Academy got
started 10 years ago.

00:21:26.040 --> 00:21:28.090
It's a nice walk in closet,
it actually has good lighting.

00:21:28.090 --> 00:21:29.270
I'm looking at it a window there,

00:21:29.270 --> 00:21:31.410
we have a nice little garden out here.

00:21:31.410 --> 00:21:33.110
So you shouldn't feel too bad for me

00:21:33.110 --> 00:21:34.750
that I'm operating out of a closet.

00:21:34.750 --> 00:21:36.610
Yeah, but there's just
a ton of books here.

00:21:36.610 --> 00:21:40.330
I mean, these are a lot
of science fiction books,

00:21:40.330 --> 00:21:42.400
got the foundation series,

00:21:42.400 --> 00:21:44.560
We've got a little Robert Heinlein here

00:21:44.560 --> 00:21:47.090
we got all around Ron
Hubbard battlefield Earth.

00:21:47.090 --> 00:21:50.320
We've got some Orson
Scott Card, Greg Bear,

00:21:50.320 --> 00:21:55.200
up here I have some philosophical
and religious books.

00:21:55.200 --> 00:21:57.470
I've got the Bhagavad Gita there,

00:21:57.470 --> 00:21:59.755
I've got some passage meditation,

00:21:59.755 --> 00:22:04.310
I have a translation of the Quran,

00:22:04.310 --> 00:22:05.143
I have...

00:22:05.143 --> 00:22:07.764
Let's see, I have a Bible
someplace over here,

00:22:07.764 --> 00:22:09.880
so I've got some of that stuff.

00:22:09.880 --> 00:22:12.400
Let's see, that's just some nonfiction.

00:22:12.400 --> 00:22:15.130
My wife's medical textbooks

00:22:15.130 --> 00:22:16.950
for medical school right over here.

00:22:16.950 --> 00:22:21.950
So yes, that's the walkthrough
through our closet library.

00:22:24.530 --> 00:22:27.187
So from YouTube, A asks,

00:22:27.187 --> 00:22:30.070
"Hey Sal, I just want to know
how to organize my time again,

00:22:30.070 --> 00:22:31.390
I mean, because of this pandemic

00:22:31.390 --> 00:22:35.360
I'm unable to study
like I used to before."

00:22:35.360 --> 00:22:38.990
Well A, what I would say is

00:22:38.990 --> 00:22:41.060
structure your day as much as possible

00:22:41.060 --> 00:22:43.170
and try to Front Load your activity.

00:22:43.170 --> 00:22:45.063
So what I do is,

00:22:45.063 --> 00:22:47.120
it's a good habit to just
try to wake up everyday

00:22:47.120 --> 00:22:49.150
at the same time, my body's so used to it

00:22:49.150 --> 00:22:50.350
I don't even need an alarm clock,

00:22:50.350 --> 00:22:53.500
my eyes just kind of open
(mumbles) at 7:00 AM.

00:22:53.500 --> 00:22:56.044
It might just be because
I'm old now, but...

00:22:56.044 --> 00:22:56.950
(mumbles)

00:22:56.950 --> 00:22:58.120
And obviously for that to happen

00:22:58.120 --> 00:22:59.930
go to sleep at a reasonable time.

00:22:59.930 --> 00:23:02.450
And I try to front load, you know,

00:23:02.450 --> 00:23:05.170
first thing I try to do things
that give me some easy wins.

00:23:05.170 --> 00:23:06.887
I make my bed, and you might say,

00:23:06.887 --> 00:23:08.490
"I don't care if my beds made or not."

00:23:08.490 --> 00:23:09.323
And I'll say,

00:23:09.323 --> 00:23:11.910
"First of all, it is very nice at night

00:23:11.910 --> 00:23:14.730
to get into a well made bed
where the sheets are nice

00:23:14.730 --> 00:23:16.882
and uncreased and all of that."

00:23:16.882 --> 00:23:19.320
It's a nice thing to have in life,

00:23:19.320 --> 00:23:21.020
but even more valuable than that

00:23:21.020 --> 00:23:23.310
it takes you about three,
four minutes to make the bed.

00:23:23.310 --> 00:23:24.337
And by doing that, you're like,

00:23:24.337 --> 00:23:26.930
"Okay I got a win, this
is gonna be a good day."

00:23:26.930 --> 00:23:29.040
And then I have a little bit
of a ritual in the morning,

00:23:29.040 --> 00:23:32.580
I like to meditate at least
15 minutes in the morning,

00:23:32.580 --> 00:23:33.413
maybe 30.

00:23:34.270 --> 00:23:35.870
That just clears my mind,

00:23:35.870 --> 00:23:38.060
it gets me a really
positive frame of mind,

00:23:38.060 --> 00:23:40.920
all my stresses seem to kind
of melt away when I do that.

00:23:40.920 --> 00:23:42.780
Then I try to do some physical activity,

00:23:42.780 --> 00:23:43.960
I mix it up a little bit,

00:23:43.960 --> 00:23:45.490
I might go for a mile run,

00:23:45.490 --> 00:23:47.087
I might do some handstand push ups,

00:23:47.087 --> 00:23:49.040
or go do some pull ups in our garage,

00:23:49.040 --> 00:23:50.860
I have a little pull up bar there.

00:23:50.860 --> 00:23:52.200
And then I get ready,

00:23:52.200 --> 00:23:54.960
and then if I...

00:23:54.960 --> 00:23:57.360
Well, COVID or not I try
to do some of the stuff

00:23:57.360 --> 00:23:59.150
that takes a little bit
more cognitive work for me.

00:23:59.150 --> 00:24:02.950
So, videos take a lot of mental processing

00:24:02.950 --> 00:24:04.560
and so I try to do those in the morning,

00:24:04.560 --> 00:24:05.687
and I always tell myself,

00:24:05.687 --> 00:24:08.840
"Hey Sal, you got 40 minutes
before your next meeting

00:24:08.840 --> 00:24:10.650
just try to accomplish
this one or two things,

00:24:10.650 --> 00:24:12.890
and if you accomplish that
give yourself a break."

00:24:12.890 --> 00:24:15.630
And then I keep doing
that throughout my day.

00:24:15.630 --> 00:24:18.600
And then I'm not hard
on myself if by the...

00:24:18.600 --> 00:24:21.607
And I used to be frankly, by
the late afternoon if I'm like,

00:24:21.607 --> 00:24:23.510
"Hey, you know, I got a lot done Sal,

00:24:23.510 --> 00:24:25.230
why don't you go for why
don't you go for a walk,

00:24:25.230 --> 00:24:26.440
take it easy a little bit."

00:24:26.440 --> 00:24:28.420
I have to admit, before
I had trouble doing that,

00:24:28.420 --> 00:24:30.440
I would just keep working,
working, working, working

00:24:30.440 --> 00:24:32.340
until I'm just kind of like so brain...

00:24:32.340 --> 00:24:35.400
My brain is so fried that
I can't do anything else,

00:24:35.400 --> 00:24:36.820
and that's not so healthy

00:24:36.820 --> 00:24:39.840
and especially in this time of
COVID and social distancing.

00:24:39.840 --> 00:24:41.090
Another thing that I'm doing,

00:24:41.090 --> 00:24:42.440
I know this isn't the
question you've asked

00:24:42.440 --> 00:24:45.460
you asked about how to use
structure or things like that,

00:24:45.460 --> 00:24:47.320
but another thing that I've been doing is

00:24:47.320 --> 00:24:49.810
as much as possible when I have meetings,

00:24:49.810 --> 00:24:51.780
I try to go on walks.

00:24:51.780 --> 00:24:52.820
It just gets fresh air

00:24:52.820 --> 00:24:55.393
and it gets my my blood
flowing to the brain.

00:24:56.600 --> 00:24:59.587
So let's see, Ricky R asks,

00:24:59.587 --> 00:25:02.250
"Hi Sal, any advice on
where I can find a checklist

00:25:02.250 --> 00:25:07.120
of what my child should know
before entering fourth grade?"

00:25:07.120 --> 00:25:09.177
So, there's two ways to think about it

00:25:09.177 --> 00:25:10.040
and actually I just...

00:25:10.040 --> 00:25:11.390
That question makes me realize

00:25:11.390 --> 00:25:13.300
that I didn't answer an
earlier question about

00:25:13.300 --> 00:25:14.603
how do you remediate students

00:25:14.603 --> 00:25:17.670
from that teacher who
teaches algebra students.

00:25:17.670 --> 00:25:19.040
So today, what I would do

00:25:19.040 --> 00:25:21.540
if a student's about
to enter fourth grade,

00:25:21.540 --> 00:25:22.970
I would have them either...

00:25:22.970 --> 00:25:24.630
I would have them work
on our third grade course

00:25:24.630 --> 00:25:25.850
on Khan Academy,

00:25:25.850 --> 00:25:27.050
and they can accelerate

00:25:27.050 --> 00:25:29.360
by taking the course
challenges in the unit tests,

00:25:29.360 --> 00:25:32.480
but if they get through the
third grade course completely,

00:25:32.480 --> 00:25:34.720
they're gonna be very
ready for fourth grade.

00:25:34.720 --> 00:25:37.080
Even better is if they get
started on the fourth grade

00:25:37.080 --> 00:25:38.820
because then when they
see it next year in school

00:25:38.820 --> 00:25:41.160
it'll be kind of they've
already been exposed to it,

00:25:41.160 --> 00:25:42.750
and so they'll have more of a cushion

00:25:42.750 --> 00:25:44.060
in case some of those concepts

00:25:44.060 --> 00:25:46.510
are a little bit more
difficult to understand.

00:25:46.510 --> 00:25:48.330
Now one thing that I said
I was gonna talk about,

00:25:48.330 --> 00:25:50.080
but I didn't but now I will,

00:25:50.080 --> 00:25:51.790
is we are actively working on

00:25:51.790 --> 00:25:53.810
what we're calling foundations courses.

00:25:53.810 --> 00:25:56.090
So there's gonna be a
foundations for fourth grade,

00:25:56.090 --> 00:25:58.420
there's gonna be a
foundations for algebra,

00:25:58.420 --> 00:26:01.780
a foundations for geometry, a
foundation's for everything,

00:26:01.780 --> 00:26:04.240
for all of our courses,
each of our grade levels.

00:26:04.240 --> 00:26:07.590
And what that's gonna be is a short course

00:26:07.590 --> 00:26:10.190
that covers all of the prerequisite skills

00:26:10.190 --> 00:26:13.620
that you need to engage
in that grade level,

00:26:13.620 --> 00:26:15.670
and the reason why that's
a little bit different

00:26:15.670 --> 00:26:18.330
than just going through
third grade is that

00:26:18.330 --> 00:26:20.550
you actually don't need
every third grade standard

00:26:20.550 --> 00:26:22.740
to necessarily engage in fourth grade,

00:26:22.740 --> 00:26:23.930
and there's actually certain

00:26:23.930 --> 00:26:25.630
maybe even second and
first grade standards

00:26:25.630 --> 00:26:28.250
that you do need to
engage in fourth grade.

00:26:28.250 --> 00:26:30.470
And so this is going to be a new course,

00:26:30.470 --> 00:26:32.130
type of course that we're gonna curate.

00:26:32.130 --> 00:26:34.450
And you can imagine most fourth graders

00:26:34.450 --> 00:26:36.020
might know a lot of that material,

00:26:36.020 --> 00:26:38.430
and so they can take the course
challenge and the unit test

00:26:38.430 --> 00:26:39.590
to accelerate through it,

00:26:39.590 --> 00:26:41.032
but then the material they don't know

00:26:41.032 --> 00:26:42.680
they'll be able to fill in those gaps

00:26:42.680 --> 00:26:45.540
and then there'll be super
prepared for fourth grade.

00:26:45.540 --> 00:26:49.360
So today, use the grade
level before on Khan Academy,

00:26:49.360 --> 00:26:51.180
get your kids to master that

00:26:51.180 --> 00:26:53.680
and they can accelerate with
course challenges in unit test.

00:26:53.680 --> 00:26:56.490
You might also want to
use the arithmetic course,

00:26:56.490 --> 00:26:58.420
and hopefully in about two months

00:26:58.420 --> 00:27:00.800
expect to see these foundations courses,

00:27:00.800 --> 00:27:04.030
which are kind of exactly
what students need

00:27:04.030 --> 00:27:06.150
to get to grade level
as quickly as possible

00:27:06.150 --> 00:27:06.983
or at least to know...

00:27:06.983 --> 00:27:08.940
At least feel good that
they have the foundations

00:27:08.940 --> 00:27:11.823
to be prepared for grade
level as quickly as possible.

00:27:13.130 --> 00:27:18.127
So from YouTube Quacker Diesels asks,

00:27:18.127 --> 00:27:20.380
"Hey Sal, how does the Khan Academy staff

00:27:20.380 --> 00:27:22.693
coping up with this crisis?"

00:27:22.693 --> 00:27:24.274
It's great question Quacker.

00:27:24.274 --> 00:27:26.441
(mumbles)

00:27:27.820 --> 00:27:30.330
I would say overall reasonably well,

00:27:30.330 --> 00:27:33.310
we were already a reasonably
distributed workforce.

00:27:33.310 --> 00:27:35.950
We had about 35, 40% of our team members

00:27:35.950 --> 00:27:39.270
were not based in our
office here in California,

00:27:39.270 --> 00:27:40.820
and so we always had a...

00:27:40.820 --> 00:27:44.540
We were always using Google
Meet and Zoom for our meetings,

00:27:44.540 --> 00:27:45.630
and we always, you know,

00:27:45.630 --> 00:27:49.140
in almost every meeting we
almost always had someone

00:27:49.140 --> 00:27:51.940
who was distributed or remote,

00:27:51.940 --> 00:27:54.250
and so that was a pretty
easy transition for us

00:27:54.250 --> 00:27:55.470
to stay functional.

00:27:55.470 --> 00:27:57.940
We just leaned heavier on those tools.

00:27:57.940 --> 00:28:00.290
I think the difficult part is

00:28:00.290 --> 00:28:01.950
people are going through stuff,

00:28:01.950 --> 00:28:03.960
they're worried about
their family members,

00:28:03.960 --> 00:28:06.850
I haven't heard of any
of our team members yet

00:28:06.850 --> 00:28:11.740
with a family member who's
gotten very sick from COVID, yet.

00:28:11.740 --> 00:28:14.740
Thank goodness, but you can
imagine people are worried.

00:28:14.740 --> 00:28:17.550
I think the biggest drain
has been on a lot of parents

00:28:17.550 --> 00:28:18.970
of young kids.

00:28:18.970 --> 00:28:23.190
I'm in that bucket, but I'm
lucky I have myself, my wife,

00:28:23.190 --> 00:28:25.090
my mother in law, we're
all in the house together.

00:28:25.090 --> 00:28:26.310
My kids...

00:28:26.310 --> 00:28:28.640
As teachers have been
doing an amazing heroic job

00:28:28.640 --> 00:28:31.130
at KLS, keeping the kids supported

00:28:31.130 --> 00:28:32.420
and keeping them learning.

00:28:32.420 --> 00:28:34.097
So I feel very fortunate there,

00:28:34.097 --> 00:28:36.150
but we have team members that

00:28:36.150 --> 00:28:38.160
they might be a single parent,

00:28:38.160 --> 00:28:40.110
they have a two year old
or they have two kids

00:28:40.110 --> 00:28:43.450
or they might be (mumbles)
both them and their partner,

00:28:43.450 --> 00:28:45.270
but they have two or three young children.

00:28:45.270 --> 00:28:47.031
You can imagine it's very hard

00:28:47.031 --> 00:28:50.470
to keep the two or three year old occupied

00:28:50.470 --> 00:28:52.200
while the same time you're trying to work,

00:28:52.200 --> 00:28:54.640
so that has definitely
caused some stress for folks.

00:28:54.640 --> 00:28:56.890
And we've been very clear
with our team members

00:28:56.890 --> 00:29:00.130
that if you can try to do it, you can.

00:29:00.130 --> 00:29:03.440
This is Khan Academy's moment
to step up for the world,

00:29:03.440 --> 00:29:06.400
but this is also a time that we, you know,

00:29:06.400 --> 00:29:08.540
some of these team members
have done such incredible work

00:29:08.540 --> 00:29:10.540
over the years, we're
not going to, you know...

00:29:10.540 --> 00:29:11.710
They have to take care of themselves

00:29:11.710 --> 00:29:13.240
and their families first and foremost,

00:29:13.240 --> 00:29:16.350
but for the most part I
think the Khan Academy team

00:29:16.350 --> 00:29:19.830
is doing well, thanks for asking.

00:29:19.830 --> 00:29:22.073
So from YouTube.

00:29:24.580 --> 00:29:25.640
If YouTube...

00:29:25.640 --> 00:29:27.840
Okay, this is a real time math problem.

00:29:27.840 --> 00:29:28.877
Avinash Carr says,

00:29:28.877 --> 00:29:31.200
"A popular website requires
users to create a password

00:29:31.200 --> 00:29:32.760
consisting of digits only.

00:29:32.760 --> 00:29:34.510
If no digit may be repeated

00:29:34.510 --> 00:29:37.630
and each word, password
must be nine digits long.

00:29:37.630 --> 00:29:39.687
How many passwords are possible?"

00:29:41.030 --> 00:29:41.863
This is...

00:29:41.863 --> 00:29:42.696
I was a little worried,

00:29:42.696 --> 00:29:43.730
I thought you're gonna give me a hard one,

00:29:43.730 --> 00:29:46.730
but I'm probably jinxing it
because I'm probably gonna

00:29:46.730 --> 00:29:50.520
in real time make a mistake, but no.

00:29:50.520 --> 00:29:53.560
So if I understand correctly, nine digits,

00:29:53.560 --> 00:29:55.120
you're not you can't repeat it.

00:29:55.120 --> 00:29:56.170
So the way I think about it,

00:29:56.170 --> 00:29:58.300
think about the leftmost digit.

00:29:58.300 --> 00:30:01.140
There are 10 points possible digits,

00:30:01.140 --> 00:30:04.700
so there's 10 possibilities
for that leftmost one.

00:30:04.700 --> 00:30:08.400
And then for the one to the right of that

00:30:08.400 --> 00:30:11.570
you've already used one
of the 10 possible digits,

00:30:11.570 --> 00:30:14.070
so now you have nine
possibilities for the next one,

00:30:14.070 --> 00:30:16.280
and then you have eight
possibilities for the next one,

00:30:16.280 --> 00:30:19.500
and so it's gonna be 10 times
nine times eight times seven

00:30:19.500 --> 00:30:23.590
and so you're gonna do that
all the way down to two.

00:30:23.590 --> 00:30:25.773
So, if I did that right it's gonna be...

00:30:25.773 --> 00:30:28.140
Because the last one you're
gonna have two digits left

00:30:28.140 --> 00:30:31.093
because you're using a nine digits long.

00:30:31.960 --> 00:30:34.060
And I'm assuming we're going
with nine digit passwords,

00:30:34.060 --> 00:30:35.070
obviously...

00:30:35.070 --> 00:30:36.600
Well, I can see how it could be trick

00:30:36.600 --> 00:30:38.920
because you say it has to be
at least nine digits long,

00:30:38.920 --> 00:30:41.400
so you're saying it
could also be 10 digits.

00:30:41.400 --> 00:30:43.330
So if I were to say just a nine digit

00:30:43.330 --> 00:30:45.340
how many nine digit passwords are there,

00:30:45.340 --> 00:30:50.340
it would be it would be 10 factorial,

00:30:50.460 --> 00:30:55.460
and then how many 10
digit passwords are there?

00:30:55.720 --> 00:30:59.530
Well, for each of those
nine digit passwords

00:30:59.530 --> 00:31:02.960
it would kind of determine
what that last digit has to be.

00:31:02.960 --> 00:31:05.650
So, you would then get an extra password

00:31:05.650 --> 00:31:08.180
essentially for each of
those nine digit passwords.

00:31:08.180 --> 00:31:11.320
So, I think you would get
two times 10 factorial

00:31:11.320 --> 00:31:12.160
is the answer,

00:31:12.160 --> 00:31:14.350
and you obviously can't have
more than a 10 digit password

00:31:14.350 --> 00:31:15.860
'cause you can't reuse the digits.

00:31:15.860 --> 00:31:18.030
Don't know if I did that, right.

00:31:18.030 --> 00:31:19.930
But here's to making ourselves vulnerable

00:31:19.930 --> 00:31:22.280
and answering math questions in real time.

00:31:22.280 --> 00:31:23.944
All right. (laughs)

00:31:23.944 --> 00:31:27.190
Maybe I'll do one more
question to redeem myself

00:31:27.190 --> 00:31:29.440
just in case I made a
careless error on that one.

00:31:29.440 --> 00:31:30.690
From Facebook,

00:31:30.690 --> 00:31:32.530
And y'all see I'm reading this real time

00:31:32.530 --> 00:31:33.720
there's no prep in this,

00:31:33.720 --> 00:31:36.250
so y'all are getting me as I am.

00:31:36.250 --> 00:31:38.487
So by Enza Charity says,

00:31:38.487 --> 00:31:40.580
"Hi Sal, how can I study better

00:31:40.580 --> 00:31:41.990
while waking up early in the morning

00:31:41.990 --> 00:31:43.430
without getting distracted?

00:31:43.430 --> 00:31:45.330
How can I also make my
studying more interesting

00:31:45.330 --> 00:31:47.137
while enjoying and understanding?"

00:31:49.190 --> 00:31:50.880
With the distraction problem,

00:31:50.880 --> 00:31:53.860
well there's a couple of
things, meditation is great.

00:31:53.860 --> 00:31:56.590
Your mind just gets a little
bit trained to not get

00:31:59.470 --> 00:32:02.770
scatterbrained I guess is
one way to think about it.

00:32:02.770 --> 00:32:05.560
I think the morning is
the best time to do it,

00:32:05.560 --> 00:32:06.780
try to find a quiet time.

00:32:06.780 --> 00:32:07.880
And I would... (mumbles)

00:32:07.880 --> 00:32:09.900
We talked about the Pomodoro
Technique in the past,

00:32:09.900 --> 00:32:12.070
which is, it's called the
Pomodoro 'cause it's named

00:32:12.070 --> 00:32:15.610
after a timer shaped
like a pomodoro tomato.

00:32:15.610 --> 00:32:16.870
But give yourself 20 minutes to say

00:32:16.870 --> 00:32:18.620
for this 20 minutes I'm gonna focus

00:32:18.620 --> 00:32:19.610
and do what I need to do,

00:32:19.610 --> 00:32:21.280
and then I'm gonna give
myself a 10 minute break,

00:32:21.280 --> 00:32:23.600
20 minutes focus and then
give myself a 10 minute break.

00:32:23.600 --> 00:32:25.010
And there's actually
some research on that,

00:32:25.010 --> 00:32:27.360
that that tends to ease procrastination

00:32:27.360 --> 00:32:29.210
can allow you to get a
little bit more focused,

00:32:29.210 --> 00:32:32.870
while also being able to recharge.

00:32:32.870 --> 00:32:36.010
You know, how did you make
study more interesting?

00:32:36.010 --> 00:32:38.580
What I would say, you have
an opportunity right now.

00:32:38.580 --> 00:32:40.140
During the normal school year

00:32:40.140 --> 00:32:42.010
you have so many things on your plate

00:32:42.010 --> 00:32:43.927
and extracurricular
activities that you're like,

00:32:43.927 --> 00:32:46.310
"Oh, I just have to cram this
so that I can do my homework

00:32:46.310 --> 00:32:47.390
or so that I can learn for tomorrow."

00:32:47.390 --> 00:32:49.000
And so you don't have the time and space

00:32:49.000 --> 00:32:52.120
to ponder and to really enjoy the concept.

00:32:52.120 --> 00:32:54.870
I hope that now that certain
things have been taken off

00:32:54.870 --> 00:32:57.180
of our plates, social things, et cetera.

00:32:57.180 --> 00:32:58.620
There's more time to ponder

00:32:58.620 --> 00:33:00.490
and the pondering is the fun part.

00:33:00.490 --> 00:33:02.440
So when you're reading, you know,

00:33:02.440 --> 00:33:04.300
I don't know what you're
trying to study, chemistry,

00:33:04.300 --> 00:33:05.269
and you're like,

00:33:05.269 --> 00:33:06.550
"Okay, PV equals nRT."

00:33:06.550 --> 00:33:08.460
Instead of just memorizing that,

00:33:08.460 --> 00:33:10.080
why does that make sense?

00:33:10.080 --> 00:33:11.380
All right, well let's just imagine

00:33:11.380 --> 00:33:13.370
N represents the number of molecules.

00:33:13.370 --> 00:33:14.350
I mean it's measured in moles,

00:33:14.350 --> 00:33:16.100
but that's just a quantity of molecules.

00:33:16.100 --> 00:33:21.100
R is just a constant (mumbles)
to make the unit's workout,

00:33:21.680 --> 00:33:23.050
and then T is temperature.

00:33:23.050 --> 00:33:25.350
So the number of molecules
and the temperature,

00:33:25.350 --> 00:33:27.730
see the more molecules,
the higher temperature,

00:33:27.730 --> 00:33:29.720
they're bouncing around more vigorously.

00:33:29.720 --> 00:33:31.800
Yeah, either if I hold volume constant

00:33:31.800 --> 00:33:33.250
it's gonna create more pressure,

00:33:33.250 --> 00:33:34.940
or if I hold pressure constant

00:33:34.940 --> 00:33:37.320
in order to do that I'd
have to expand the volume.

00:33:37.320 --> 00:33:39.154
Or if I hold pressure constant

00:33:39.154 --> 00:33:41.957
the balloon would blow up so
to speak, and you're like,

00:33:41.957 --> 00:33:44.457
"Oh, that makes a lot of
sense, this is intuitive.

00:33:45.430 --> 00:33:47.350
Of course PV equals nRT."

00:33:47.350 --> 00:33:48.500
And then it becomes exciting

00:33:48.500 --> 00:33:50.720
and then when you look around the world

00:33:50.720 --> 00:33:52.070
all sorts of things make sense,

00:33:52.070 --> 00:33:53.840
and you start to see it in the real world.

00:33:53.840 --> 00:33:57.430
I remember the famously
Tom Brady in Deflategate,

00:33:57.430 --> 00:33:59.690
there was this debate
about whether his footballs

00:33:59.690 --> 00:34:02.950
were deflated inappropriately.

00:34:02.950 --> 00:34:05.330
That was actually a PV equals nRT problem,

00:34:05.330 --> 00:34:06.740
and I made some videos on that

00:34:06.740 --> 00:34:08.370
and it's still under debate,

00:34:08.370 --> 00:34:10.430
but you start seeing these things

00:34:10.430 --> 00:34:12.700
that you think are these
random academic formulas,

00:34:12.700 --> 00:34:14.380
you start seeing them in the world

00:34:14.380 --> 00:34:15.650
when you start pondering them,

00:34:15.650 --> 00:34:17.390
and you start making connections

00:34:17.390 --> 00:34:19.250
to other concepts in your life.

00:34:19.250 --> 00:34:20.970
You know, what I remind every student is

00:34:20.970 --> 00:34:23.910
what you get to learn
today on Khan Academy,

00:34:23.910 --> 00:34:25.890
from your textbook from your teacher,

00:34:25.890 --> 00:34:28.010
most of these things are the culmination

00:34:28.010 --> 00:34:29.570
of someone's life work

00:34:29.570 --> 00:34:32.970
or often times the combination
of many people's life works

00:34:32.970 --> 00:34:36.010
over hundreds of years
and you get the answer.

00:34:36.010 --> 00:34:39.350
Isaac Newton, Marie Curie,
they would have killed

00:34:39.350 --> 00:34:43.270
to have your physics or
chemistry or biology textbook,

00:34:43.270 --> 00:34:46.040
it would have saved them a
lot of work, and you get it.

00:34:46.040 --> 00:34:47.810
And so realize that,

00:34:47.810 --> 00:34:50.040
realize that there are profound answers.

00:34:50.040 --> 00:34:54.190
I mean, the nature of DNA,
these are mind blowing things

00:34:54.190 --> 00:34:57.080
that Watson and Crick would have loved,

00:34:57.080 --> 00:34:59.520
Mendel would have loved
to get their hands on

00:34:59.520 --> 00:35:00.610
and you get those answers.

00:35:00.610 --> 00:35:04.053
So look at it in that lens
you're getting the answers,

00:35:05.250 --> 00:35:07.810
And do them justice, think
about them, ponder them,

00:35:07.810 --> 00:35:09.110
don't just try to memorize

00:35:09.110 --> 00:35:11.440
and regurgitate it for the next test.

00:35:11.440 --> 00:35:14.883
So that's my advice, And I
realized I'm all out of time.

00:35:16.590 --> 00:35:19.620
This was a fun one, and I...

